<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gangeyadeva</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1015–1041</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gangeyadeva was a king of the Kalachuris of Tripuri in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the early 11th century. He took the title Vikramaditya and expanded the kingdom northwards, extending his rule as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>. His gold coins, showing a seated goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>, were widely imitated by later dynasties of northern India. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmikarna">Lakshmikarna</a> brought the kingdom to its height.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gangeyadeva.jpg" alt="Gangeyadeva" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalachuris_of_Tripuri._Gangeya._Circa_1015-1040._Lakshmi_seated_facing_-_%E2%80%9C%C5%9Arimad_Gangeya_Devah%E2%80%9D_in_three_lines.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
